         "1. Definition: any motion that repeats identically after a fixed time interval T.\n2. Classical examples:\n   - Oscillation of a simple pendulum in a grandfather clock.\n   - Motion of a child swinging back and forth on a playground swing.\n   - Plucking a taut steel guitar string (vibrational periodic motion).\n   - Bouncing of a mass hanging from an elastic coil spring.\n3. The timekeeping connection: Galileo discovered that a pendulum of fixed length takes the exact same time for every swing (isochronism).\n4. Astronomical periodicity: Earth revolving around the Sun once every 365.25 days provides our definition of a year; Earth rotating on its axis once every 24 hours defines our day.\n5. Conclude: invariant temporal intervals in periodic motion provide humanity's universal master clocks.",
