         "Parametric kinematics of cycloidal motion: x(t) = R*(omega*t - sin(omega*t)), y(t) = R*(1 - cos(omega*t)). The contact point has instantaneous zero velocity (cusp v = 0), while the topmost point moves at 2 * v_cm.",
         "1. The common intuition trap: because the wheel is round and spinning, students assume a point on the rim traces a circle in space.\n2. Ground-frame reality: the wheel does not spin in place—its axle moves forward at velocity v_cm.\n3. Deconstruct the path of the LED:\n   - When the LED touches the asphalt: it has zero instantaneous velocity (stationary contact point to prevent skidding).\n   - As the wheel rolls forward: the LED lifts up and accelerates forward.\n   - At the topmost point of the wheel: the LED is moving forward at TWICE the speed of the bicycle (2 * v_cm)!\n   - As it rolls down: it swoops down in a smooth arch to touch the ground again.\n4. Geometry: this arching trajectory is a famous mathematical curve called a cycloid.\n5. Pedagogical takeaway: composite motion creates emergent trajectories that are entirely different from the individual component motions.",
