         "1. Classical continuous space: classical geometry assumes any line segment can be divided by 2 forever into infinitely tiny pieces.\n2. Quantum reality: physical measurement requires photons of light to probe position.\n3. The Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle: to measure a smaller distance Delta x, you must use light of shorter wavelength lambda and higher energy E = h * c / lambda.\n4. The black hole threshold: if you concentrate enough energy into a space smaller than 1.6 * 10^-35 metres (the Planck length), the energy density becomes so colossal that it forms a microscopic black hole!\n5. The measurement paradox: instead of revealing smaller details, the micro black hole swallows your probe photon and prevents any signal from escaping.\n6. Conclusion: the Planck length represents the fundamental 'pixel size' of physical spacetime in our universe.",
